ive  where an author is abridging  plagiarizing adapting an earlier an  accident work and the plaintiff wins in  all of these cases the defendant is  unable to defend a derivative  publication best forward to 1741 and  there's a very prominent case Giles V  Wilcox which is fought over a  publication by Fletcher Giles that we  just saw  so John Wilcox said basically abridged  Giles is text and published it and Giles  was understandably upset he took Wilcox  to court and Wilcox defended his  publication on the basis that he had  liked highly manipulated the source text  he had transformed in a number of ways  he wasn't going to influence the  marketability of the original text etc  etc and so his derivative publication  was identified as a distinct  copyrightable work and was found not  infringing and so this precedent in 1741  opens up the opportunity for a number of  subsequent cases all of which have  derivative artistic text reuse that are  defensible that are found non-infringing  so this is a really sort of watershed  moment in history I mean this is the  creation of fair use copyright law and  said in 1741 at least four texts in the  image domain there was no fair use law  until a very sort of breakthrough 1785  case say anymore and  based on sayers and this was proven in  the case in the case and that becomes  like an artistic trick artists would  deliberately encode false information  into their into their pictorial graph so  that if they were sort of reprinted they  could identify those telltale marks kind  of clever but in any event because this  was a C chart and because Moore's math  made a number of improvements on the  plaintiffs mouth he his map was actually  found non-infringing it was found to be  a defensible work and so this creates  precedent for a number of subsequent  cases all of which revolve around  derivative image production where a  derivative image is found to be  non-infringing and so the research  question that that this part of my work