ually very  little work on measuring like  probability that  a given work would have disappeared or  this measuring survival rates gathered  from over 2,200 libraries worldwide we  can look at just to see the library that  were basically on the verge of becoming  extinct a significant portion of sort of  book history is just absolutely missing  and can't be part of the study but we've  got decent decent numbers  whereas later the century kind of  hotbeds of reprinting because they  weren't subject to English copyright law  provisions so printers in Edinboro and  in Dublin can reprint sort of you know  wholesale they can sell records in  London they deliberately misidentify  title pages they lie and say that a  record was published in London because  London publications were sort of hotter  commodities and then there are kind of  intricacies within these different  geographic locations so for instance if  we look at nicer type intended to be  preserved  and presses they have old typeface and  so although in brown dublin are kind of  again these hotbeds of reprinting we  can't capture as much from them because  the signal is less strong so a lot of  problems with the actual data that's  said we'll begin by looking at patterns  in derivative text reuse and mean line  they're drawn roughly in the 80% region  so this is to say that the text reuse  algorithm needs to be sufficiently fuzzy  in order to work with these characters  we can't count on perfect sort of word  level transcription rates and so the  technique used for this part of the  study is called the min hash algorithm  which allows us to analyze text  sequences and identify these hashes  these sort of derive these  characteristics of the text such that we  can compare the characteristics of two  passages and identify what with a  certain probability you know whether or  not they match and so the basic notion  here is  we're going to we're going to  in which that sequence of values occurs  in the vector for the